From 9233e9c4c4b8db85616682174e4e41e4effcddcc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Theorem Labs
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 04:51:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [compiler-rt] sealed init: madvise(MADV_DONTNEED) for real,
 and nothing else

Sealed init dropped every madvise at internal_madvise, on the argument that
all of it is advice: MADV_DONTNEED to hand freed pages back, MADV_DONTDUMP
and MADV_[NO]HUGEPAGE on the shadow.  What that cost was not only resident
memory.  In the model these binaries are verified against, a page of the
MAP_NORESERVE shadow is charged to the run's budget when first touched and,
with no madvise, never given back, so a program that allocates and frees
large blocks under heavy traffic exhausts a budget its plain build does not
(protobuf-c and harfbuzz, 64MiB inputs, a 512GiB budget).

The one advice that returns pages is now issued
for real again.  internal_madvise passes MADV_DONTNEED through and still
drops every other advice, so the runtime makes the call from
ReleaseMemoryPagesToOS -- on page-aligned ranges, inside mappings it made
itself: the shadow of freed chunks (FlushUnneededASanShadowMemory from the
allocator's unmap callback), of freed thread stacks and of fake stacks --
and nothing else of the kind.  The specification gains the matching arm,
madvise(addr, len, MADV_DONTNEED) on a page-aligned range inside a live
anonymous mapping: returns 0, charges returned.  DONTDUMP and HUGEPAGE, which
nothing models, stay dropped.  The allocator's own release-to-OS pass, which
would return free chunk pages in the arena, stays pinned off
(allocator_release_to_os_interval_ms = -1) because it reads the clock.

The suite's syscall gate admits madvise with MADV_DONTNEED and no other
advice value, as the one entry the runtime rather than the program
justifies, and sealed_madvise proves both halves: killed by the filter
without the entry, finished with it.
---
 compiler-rt/lib/asan/asan_flags.cpp           | 13 ++++++++++
 .../lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_linux.cpp  | 25 ++++++++++++++-----
 .../lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_sealed.h   | 12 ++++++---
 3 files changed, 41 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)

diff --git a/compiler-rt/lib/asan/asan_flags.cpp b/compiler-rt/lib/asan/asan_flags.cpp
index 7b8bbce14..6937fbb93 100644
--- a/compiler-rt/lib/asan/asan_flags.cpp
+++ b/compiler-rt/lib/asan/asan_flags.cpp
@@ -299,6 +299,19 @@ static void SealFlags() {
   cf.compress_stack_depot = 0;
   cf.print_module_map = 0;
   cf.decorate_proc_maps = false;
+  // The shadow is zeroed with memset and never re-mapped.  Above this
+  // threshold FastPoisonShadow zero-fills a shadow range by mapping fresh
+  // pages over it with MAP_FIXED instead of writing zeros, on every large
+  // allocation and every large free; in the model these binaries are
+  // verified against each of those is a new grant carved out of the shadow
+  // mapping, and the madvise(MADV_DONTNEED) that later hands the freed
+  // block's shadow back (patch 0020) may only cover a range inside ONE
+  // grant.  With the threshold at its maximum the three mappings made at
+  // start-up (SealedReserveShadow) are the only shadow grants for the life
+  // of the process, so every madvise the runtime issues lies inside one of
+  // them.  The cost is a memset of the shadow where an mmap was: 128KiB of
+  // zeros per MiB unpoisoned.
+  cf.clear_shadow_mmap_threshold = ~(uptr)0;
   OverrideCommonFlags(cf);
   // The shadow gap was protected before these flags were parsed
   // (SealedReserveShadow), so the flag that would leave it open is pinned to
diff --git a/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_linux.cpp b/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_linux.cpp
index 225c5d3bf..1ad1a5374 100644
--- a/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_linux.cpp
+++ b/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_linux.cpp
@@ -281,13 +281,26 @@ int internal_mprotect(void *addr, uptr length, int prot) {
   return internal_syscall(SYSCALL(mprotect), (uptr)addr, length, prot);
 }
 
+#  ifndef MADV_DONTNEED
+#    define MADV_DONTNEED 4
+#  endif
 int internal_madvise(uptr addr, uptr length, int advice) {
-  // Every advice this runtime gives is advice: MADV_DONTNEED to hand freed
-  // pages back, MADV_DONTDUMP and MADV_[NO]HUGEPAGE on the shadow.  Dropping
-  // them costs resident memory the allocator would have released and changes
-  // nothing else, so sealed mode drops all of them at the one place they are
-  // issued rather than at each caller.
-  if (SealedInit())
+  // Every advice this runtime gives is advice, and sealed mode used to drop
+  // all of it here.  What that cost was not only resident memory: in the
+  // model these binaries are verified against, a page of the MAP_NORESERVE
+  // shadow is charged to the run's budget when first touched and, with no
+  // madvise, never given back, so a program that allocates and frees large
+  // blocks under heavy traffic exhausts a budget its plain build does not.
+  // The one advice that returns pages, MADV_DONTNEED, is issued for real now
+  // -- from ReleaseMemoryPagesToOS, on page-aligned ranges inside mappings
+  // the runtime made itself: the shadow of freed chunks, thread stacks and
+  // fake stacks -- and the specification models exactly that call (its arm:
+  // madvise 233, MADV_DONTNEED, page-aligned, inside a live anonymous
+  // mapping, returns 0, charges returned).  The rest -- MADV_DONTDUMP and
+  // MADV_[NO]HUGEPAGE on the shadow at start-up -- nothing models, and they
+  // stay dropped.  The allocator's own release-to-OS pass stays pinned off
+  // (allocator_release_to_os_interval_ms = -1), since it reads the clock.
+  if (SealedInit() && advice != MADV_DONTNEED)
     return 0;
   return internal_syscall(SYSCALL(madvise), addr, length, advice);
 }
diff --git a/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_sealed.h b/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_sealed.h
index 66eb3e0f1..59e43e57c 100644
--- a/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_sealed.h
+++ b/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_sealed.h
@@ -23,8 +23,13 @@
 //     is not resolved either.
 //   - no signal handler is installed and no alternate signal stack is set up,
 //     so no rt_sigaction, sigaltstack or rt_sigprocmask.
-//   - no madvise, so the allocator does not return pages to the OS and the
-//     shadow is neither MADV_DONTDUMP nor MADV_NOHUGEPAGE.
+//   - madvise only with MADV_DONTNEED -- the shadow of freed memory, handed
+//     back so that the model's budget gets its charges back -- and no other
+//     advice: the shadow is neither MADV_DONTDUMP nor MADV_NOHUGEPAGE, and the
+//     allocator's own release-to-OS pass stays off, and the shadow is zeroed
+//     with memset rather than re-mapped, so that the three mappings made at
+//     start-up stay the only shadow grants and every madvise lies inside one
+//     of them.  See internal_madvise and SealFlags.
 //   - no prlimit64: the core dumper is left alone and RLIMIT_STACK is not read.
 //   - no getpid.
 //   - a detection dies by SIGABRT rather than by exit_group: abort_on_error
@@ -47,7 +52,8 @@
 //     is an abort.  See SealedHangOnAllocationFailure below.
 //
 // What remains at start-up is mmap, munmap, mprotect and the program's own
-// syscalls.  A run that reports an error is not sealed and is not meant to be:
+// syscalls, and from then on one more the program does not make, madvise
+// with MADV_DONTNEED, which the model names.  A run that reports an error is not sealed and is not meant to be:
 // symbolization and the report itself do what they always did, except for the
 // last event -- abort_on_error is pinned, so the death under a report is a
 // SIGABRT rather than an exit status the program could also have produced.
